## Reached at
  A Go package in the application module tree rooted at /app/gogs, whose module
  root is /app/gogs/go.mod. The caller reaches it by compiling a small driver
  program inside that tree and statically linking the package into it, then
  running the resulting binary as a subprocess. The link is a Go import
  resolved against the local module tree at build time:
      import pam "gogs.io/gogs/internal/auth/pam"
  Go forbids importing an `internal/` package from outside the module that
  contains it, so the caller builds from a package directory it itself creates
  directly under /app/gogs, with working directory /app/gogs and the Go
  toolchain at /usr/local/go/bin/go:
      go build -tags pam -o /tmp/vsb_pam_harness.bin ./<caller-created package>
  Each authentication attempt is then one subprocess invocation of that binary
  with three command-line arguments, bounded by a 30-second timeout.

## Must provide
  gogs.io/gogs/internal/auth/pam   a Go package, project-owned source inside
                                   the /app/gogs module tree, importable by
                                   that path from within the module
  PAMAuth                          exported function of that package; the
                                   caller depends on the signature
                                   func(service, username, password string) error
                                   and on being able to call it synchronously;
                                   it must compile and be present under the
                                   build tag `pam` (the caller always builds
                                   with -tags pam)

## Called as
  The driver calls the function exactly once per process:
      err := pam.PAMAuth(os.Args[1], os.Args[2], os.Args[3])
  PAMAuth(service, username, password)
      service   — 1st positional argument; a string naming the PAM service to
                  authenticate against
      username  — 2nd positional argument; a string
      password  — 3rd positional argument; a string
  Each input is a string taken verbatim from the caller'"'"'s arguments; callers
  pass arbitrary strings, including empty strings and long strings.

## Setup required
  - /app/gogs must be the complete application module tree with
    /app/gogs/go.mod present, so the import above resolves to project-owned
    source in that tree and the caller-created package can be compiled there.
  - The package must build under the `pam` build tag, which compiles cgo code
    against the system PAM headers; the headers and the PAM runtime are
    preinstalled in the image (preexisting dependencies, not implementation
    work).
  - The PAM service named by the first argument is defined by the preexisting
    image configuration at /etc/pam.d/gogs; that file is not implementation-
    owned.
  - The system accounts used in calls are created, modified, and removed by
    the caller'"'"'s own setup through system user-management tools; they are
    state the caller owns, not something the implementation provides.

## Test harness boundary
  The calling side owns the driver program: it creates a temporary package
  directory directly under /app/gogs, copies its own source in as main.go,
  compiles it fresh on every session with `go build -tags pam`, and removes
  the directory again after building. It runs the compiled binary as a
  subprocess, supplies the three command-line arguments, and judges each
  attempt from the process exit status and standard output. All of that — the
  driver source, the build orchestration, the subprocess invocation, and the
  result interpretation — belongs to the caller, not to the implementation;
  the engineer must not rebuild or modify any of it. The image also ships a
  prebuilt binary at /app/pam_auth_test; it is not on the reachability path
  and is never invoked by the caller — every attempt is linked fresh against
  the current source in /app/gogs, so nothing about a stale binary or any
  other preexisting executable stands between the caller and the package.
